/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77b9ea2d-e848-5bec-b67a-2f543f11e0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450463} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cab212d-a598-58be-a114-76dafe7042a6" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "632bafc0-a6e4-5e65-b220-22e1ed511764" :question/math-academy-id "q-333968" :question/problem "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =2,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$\n- **II.** The stability region is $|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$\n- **III.** The method is absolutely stable.\n- **IV.** The method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let’s check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation  \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-2{y}_{n}-2({y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{y}_{n}+4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+2{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}<Δx-\\frac{1}{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0<Δx & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, since it satisfies this inequality, the modified Euler method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and hence statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb4d0cae-0177-5a5d-8631-f39b4951db48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450464} {:answer/id #uuid "f3aff59c-97a3-5e6f-875e-4e2796a585cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3aff59c-97a3-5e6f-875e-4e2796a585cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450465} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc3cc60-3787-5506-84dd-27e14fcdcd91" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451186} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd2f5deb-038e-590b-b232-6e3475aa20c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450466} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c56064-c15b-5471-a1cb-2509a6b27af2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd2f5deb-038e-590b-b232-6e3475aa20c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450466} :answer-field/id #uuid "f67c096d-3014-5488-8e50-1ea90c5de739" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451187} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f89fa4-4b0f-54d4-9391-4960fd8fea1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450468} {:answer/id #uuid "43ef8fa4-cb17-5725-b52e-611adaac83b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23f89fa4-4b0f-54d4-9391-4960fd8fea1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450468} :answer-field/id #uuid "22117b13-f654-5ed2-83f1-db1f9066dd85" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6c60236-078a-5017-8be7-a68ef13f931d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334844" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)={x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$\n- **II.** $f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0$\n- **III.** $f=O({x}^{4})$ as $x\\to 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(1+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is finite, and $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x}+3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x\\to 0,$ the term $\\frac{1}{x}$ becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude, while $3x\\to 0.$\nSo\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x}+3x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is infinite, and $f\\ne O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{4}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x\\to 0,$ the term $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+3)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is infinite, and $f\\ne O({x}^{4})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1651c86c-e436-5f01-be30-6f86f178c122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450470} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6a0e20-73cf-5219-a0c9-9ab473ffb804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b6a0e20-73cf-5219-a0c9-9ab473ffb804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450471} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6216e9b-be5a-5902-a8e1-117bad30d0b9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451189} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb6f2da0-ea0d-5d43-8983-8c2c1ff56bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450472} {:answer/id #uuid "19e47932-b493-5280-a793-536c92023470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19e47932-b493-5280-a793-536c92023470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450473} :answer-field/id #uuid "582fe96a-f554-52fe-9758-256a3985f7c8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451190} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0961aa7-98e8-54b0-95b3-2444bb78655f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450474} {:answer/id #uuid "d0413559-97a8-5a12-813d-f509aaf67bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0961aa7-98e8-54b0-95b3-2444bb78655f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450474} :answer-field/id #uuid "7db2b150-4480-5dbc-bcd0-3611fa5ed1c5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3954ad07-64f7-58a6-b0b9-0080fcfa7904" :question/math-academy-id "q-334854" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- **II.** $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- **III.** $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(2x+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere we used the fact that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1.$ Hence, $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(2+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, near $x=0$ we know that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x≈x,$ so $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}$ behaves like $\\frac{1}{x},$ which becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(2+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050291fd-ee09-542c-ab46-8146371d0b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450476} {:answer/id #uuid "1f23d597-2b9c-5980-a71a-67034259bfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f23d597-2b9c-5980-a71a-67034259bfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450477} :answer-field/id #uuid "b67b015c-0d52-5094-8394-43e4b1410bf4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451192} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66e1ae7b-3409-51af-bd9e-b9c799d28850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450478} {:answer/id #uuid "f75b0c66-cec2-53e3-a134-a530d1cc5305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66e1ae7b-3409-51af-bd9e-b9c799d28850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450478} :answer-field/id #uuid "84be21d0-e1c3-59ea-ad50-63c0a062d31c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451193} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d057d367-93e3-5241-bc29-cc4c19df0e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450480} {:answer/id #uuid "032dd146-059c-516c-a1e5-5a280946b148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d057d367-93e3-5241-bc29-cc4c19df0e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450480} :answer-field/id #uuid "b99c49a4-cef0-5c95-8d1d-a868db0ce85a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3acb3544-bfaf-54f0-ae53-b0963dc83190" :question/math-academy-id "q-334861" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- **II.** $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- **III.** $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}≈\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3x+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈1$ near $x=0,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-3x+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157453 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb0517fc-c050-534e-a990-cc4dda2e3c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450482} {:answer/id #uuid "34a22a8d-3d1b-5063-a513-9c72fd120848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34a22a8d-3d1b-5063-a513-9c72fd120848" 