e {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21ffccea-3a10-52a2-b505-5bca2669d5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450584} :answer-field/id #uuid "aff1d921-f2fc-5adb-9de5-6f6e814e0541" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7fab40d0-11c4-5889-9e14-2d675fe9ad31" :question/math-academy-id "q-37261" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={2{n}^{2}∣n\\in N,1\\le n\\le 3}$ and $B={2,0,8,18}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A⊈B$\n- **II.** $∅⊆B$\n- **III.** $B⊆A$\n- **IV.** $A⊇B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the set $A.$ Substituting the values $n\\in {1,2,3}$ into the formula $2{n}^{2},$ we get the following elements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n=1 & \\,⇒\\,2\\cdot {1}^{2}=2 \\\\ n=2 & \\,⇒\\,2\\cdot {2}^{2}=8 \\\\ n=3 & \\,⇒\\,2\\cdot {3}^{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA={2,8,18}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊈B$ is false. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $0\\in B$ but $0\\notin A.$\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f21c4a-93c8-548f-8c4d-bb755dd17e33" :